{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution(s) to the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-8691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\pi $ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\pi .$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx=\\pi +2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\pi $ is the only solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-8691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "af16deb4f3cade911a5baf73e11bed8c89c21471125526c398c9d38d11a4d5a4"} {"question" "q-8691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-8691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x=0,\\pi "] ["math" "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x=\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-8691" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-72841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-72841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{5}) & =1.982\\,313…≈1.982\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.982\\,3$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.982\\,3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -1.982\\,3 & ≈1.159\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.159\\,3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/assets/250adbaae0378e3f42ab4340479bb4590aa7f39a1094ea39010de3d5758b4e3e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.159\\,3 \\\\  & ≈4.300\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈1.982\\,3\\cdot 4.300\\,9≈8.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-72841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "af16deb4f3cade911a5baf73e11bed8c89c21471125526c398c9d38d11a4d5a4"} {"question" "q-72841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-72841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "6.3"] ["math" "7.5"] ["math" "8.5"] ["math" "8.9"] ["math" "9.2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-72841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-72845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the solution(s) to the equation $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3}{2}=1$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-72845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3 & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)={180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${180}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={180}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={180}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x={180}^{∘}$ is the only solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-72845" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "af16deb4f3cade911a5baf73e11bed8c89c21471125526c398c9d38d11a4d5a4"} {"question" "q-72845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-72845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x={0}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={180}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={30}^{∘},\\,{210}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={60}^{∘},\\,{240}^{∘}"] ["math" "x={90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-72845" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x={180}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-120989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.9$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest ten?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-120989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.9)=25.841\\,932{…}^{∘}≈{25.84}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${25.84}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={25.84}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of \n${\\theta }_{R}={25.84}^{∘}.$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={25.84}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/assets/4c65ecb4fdcefc332b75f2b459f65ba872a2d2d0826b27ba80df1c8f8f3b606e.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{25.84}^{∘} \\\\  & ={334.16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={334.16}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{25.84}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=25.84\\cdot (-25.84)≈-670\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest ten." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-120989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "af16deb4f3cade911a5baf73e11bed8c89c21471125526c398c9d38d11a4d5a4"} {"question" "q-120989" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-120989" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-560"] ["math" "-590"] ["math" "-610"] ["math" "-650"] ["math" "-670"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-120989" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-670"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-338573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-338573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${135}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x={135}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/assets/91e4de557177693203cbbc48c12bbf7af3de1739c8405ac05bdfa06d3e92638c.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-135}^{∘}$ and $x={135}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-338573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "af16deb4f3cade911a5baf73e11bed8c89c21471125526c398c9d38d11a4d5a4"} {"question" "q-338573" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083eb7e17631524f24ae68202559711c43277b2f7736f6d81a"} {"question" "q-338573" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-135"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978101/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9d1273f69ccb1083